hypotheses.statement:
For every fixed integer $J\geq2$ and every pair of fixed phase vectors $(\zeta_k)_{k=1}^{J-1}$ and $(\xi_k)_{k=1}^{J-1}$ of absolute value one, the corresponding labels satisfy $$\label{eq:mixed}
 \lim_{x\to\infty}\frac1x\sum_{1\leq n<x}
                   \overline{g_x(n)}F_x(n+1)=0,$$ where $F_x=f_x-\mu$ and the limit is through all integer scales.

There is a function $r(H)$ tending to zero as $H\to\infty$ such that, for every real multiplicative function $g:\mathbb N\to[-1,1]$ and $2\leq H\leq X$, $$\label{eq:labels-real-MR}
 \frac1X\int_X^{2X}
   |\mathcal A_Hg(z)-\mathcal L_Xg|^2\,dz\leq r(H).$$

For every multiplicative function $g:\mathbb N\to\mathbb C$ with $|g|\leq1$ and $X\geq H\geq10$, $$\label{eq:labels-complex-quantitative}
 \frac1X\int_X^{2X}|\mathcal A_Hg(z)|^2\,dz
 \ll (1+M(g;X))e^{-M(g;X)}
       +\left(\frac{\log\log H}{\log H}\right)^2
       +(\log X)^{-1/50},$$ with an absolute implied constant.

For each fixed nonnegative integer $H$ and either of the above values of $z$, there are real constants $c_j(z)$ such that, for $Y\ge3$, $$\sum_{n\le Y}g_z(n)
 =Y\sum_{j=0}^{H}c_j(z)(\log Y)^{z-1-j}
       +O_H\bigl(Y(\log Y)^{z-2-H}\bigr),$$ where $$c_0(z)=\frac1{\Gamma(z)}
          \prod_p(1+z/p)(1-1/p)^z>0.$$

Uniformly for $0<|j|\le T$ and squarefree rough $b\asymp TX$, $$\sum_{\substack{c\asymp X\\b+jc\asymp TX,\ b+jc>0}}
               A_0(c)A_0(b+jc)\ll X\Sigma(j).
 \label{eq:6}$$ On the same fixed log-size ranges, $$\sum_{\substack{b\asymp TX,\ c\asymp X\\a=b+jc\asymp TX,\ a>0}}
               A_0(b)A_0(a)A_0(c)\ll TX^2\Sigma(j).
 \label{eq:7}$$ The implied constants do not depend on $b,j,X$, or $B$.

There is $c_5>0$, independent of all sufficiently large fixed $C_*$, for which the nonnegative energy below satisfies the displayed lower bound: $$\label{eq:15}
\begin{gathered}
 I_{2,x}
 = \frac1x\sum_{c\ge1}
   \rho_0(\log c/B)A(c)
   \sum_{m\ge1}K(m)
   \left|
     \sum_{\substack{a\ge1\\c\mid am+1}}
     A(a)K(l_a)\psi(a/(Tc))
     \phi(am/x)F_x(am+1)
   \right|^2,
 \\
 \liminf_{B\to\infty}\ \liminf_{x\to\infty}
       \frac{I_{2,x}}{BT}\ge c_5
\end{gathered}$$ Its diagonal contribution is negligible: $$\lim_{B\to\infty}\ \limsup_{x\to\infty}
       \frac{I_{2,x}^{\mathrm{diag}}}{BT}=0.$$

For every possible endpoint type $S_i$, not merely almost every typical type, and every allowed lag $j=k-i$, $$\label{eq:23}
 \mathbb E_{S_k}\mathcal L_{ik}(S_i,S_k;s)
 \ll\frac{\Sigma(j)}T.$$

For a sufficiently fine fixed regularity grid and then a sufficiently small fixed tolerance, the independent-site latent kernel satisfies $$\sum_{k\ne i}\mathbb E\,\mathcal L_{ik}^{2}\ll B^{-.21}
 \qquad (1\le i\le M).
 \label{eq:24}$$ Moreover, its normalized total mass has bounded second moment: $$\mathbb E\left(\frac1M\sum_{i,k}\mathcal L_{ik}\right)^2\ll1.$$

The grid and tolerance can be chosen so that, uniformly for $(a,b,c)\in\mathcal C_j$ and $\eta T\le |j|\le T$, $$\mathbb E^{\mathrm{tilt}}_{a,b}
   \big[
    \mathbf 1_{\mathcal T_b\ \mathrm{regular}}
    \mathbf 1_{\mathcal T_a\ \mathrm{regular}}
    N_{a,b,c}(\mathcal T_b,\mathcal T_a)
   \big]
 \ll B^{.009}.$$

For a fixed prime cutoff $P_1\ge2$, define on all integers $j$ $$\mathfrak S_{P_1}(j)
 =\prod_{\substack{p\le P_1\\p\mid j}}(1-1/p)^{-1}
   \prod_{\substack{p\le P_1\\p\nmid j}}
       \left(1-\frac1{(p-1)^2}\right).$$

outputs.exact_statement:
For every fixed $0<\alpha<\beta<\infty$, positive integer $q$, and residue class $a\pmod q$, the distribution of $$\bigl(\Omega_{\mathcal B_{k,x}}(n)\bigr)_{1\leq k<J}$$ among the integers $\alpha x<n\leq\beta x$, $n\equiv a\pmod q$, normalized to have mass one, converges as $x\to\infty$. Its limit depends only on $J$, and not on $\alpha,\beta,q,a$.*



*Consequently there is a number $\mu=\mu(J,\zeta_1,\ldots,\zeta_{J-1})$ with $|\mu|\leq1$ such that $$\label{eq:labels-marginal-mean}
 \lim_{x\to\infty}
 \frac{q}{(\beta-\alpha)x}
 \sum_{\substack{\alpha x<n\leq\beta x\\n\equiv a\pmod q}}f_x(n)
 =\mu.$$ The same assertion holds for $g_x$, with a mean $\mu_g$ depending only on $J$ and the vector $(\xi_k)_{k=1}^{J-1}$. Write $$\label{eq:labels-centered}
 F_x(n)=f_x(n)-\mu.$$ Then $|F_x(n)|\leq2$, and for every fixed positive integer $u$, $$\label{eq:labels-multiplier}
 F_x(un)=F_x(n),\qquad g_x(un)=g_x(n)\qquad(n\geq1)$$ for all sufficiently large $x$ in terms of $u,J$.

For every fixed integer $J\geq2$ and every pair of fixed phase vectors $(\zeta_k)_{k=1}^{J-1}$ and $(\xi_k)_{k=1}^{J-1}$ of absolute value one, the corresponding labels satisfy $$\label{eq:mixed}
 \lim_{x\to\infty}\frac1x\sum_{1\leq n<x}
                   \overline{g_x(n)}F_x(n+1)=0,$$ where $F_x=f_x-\mu$ and the limit is through all integer scales.

Let $T=T(B)\to\infty$, let $L_B$ be positive integers tending to infinity, and let $a_B$ be any integer for each $B$. Fix $0<s_0<s_1<\infty$, a positive integer $D$, a residue $r\pmod D$, and $v\geq0$. Then $$\label{eq:labels-short}
 \lim_{B\to\infty}\limsup_{x\to\infty}
 \frac1{Tx}\sum_{s_0Tx\leq n\leq s_1Tx}
 \left|
   \frac1{L_B}\sum_{i=a_B+1}^{a_B+L_B}
     \mathbf 1_{i\equiv r\ (D)}F_x(n+i)G_{B,v}(n+i)
 \right|^2=0.$$ For each fixed $B$ all the arguments of the arithmetic functions are positive once $x$ is sufficiently large. The same conclusion holds if the inner upper limit is taken along any sequence $x\to\infty$.

There is a function $r(H)$ tending to zero as $H\to\infty$ such that, for every real multiplicative function $g:\mathbb N\to[-1,1]$ and $2\leq H\leq X$, $$\label{eq:labels-real-MR}
 \frac1X\int_X^{2X}
   |\mathcal A_Hg(z)-\mathcal L_Xg|^2\,dz\leq r(H).$$

For every multiplicative function $g:\mathbb N\to\mathbb C$ with $|g|\leq1$ and $X\geq H\geq10$, $$\label{eq:labels-complex-quantitative}
 \frac1X\int_X^{2X}|\mathcal A_Hg(z)|^2\,dz
 \ll (1+M(g;X))e^{-M(g;X)}
       +\left(\frac{\log\log H}{\log H}\right)^2
       +(\log X)^{-1/50},$$ with an absolute implied constant.

Fix $J\geq2$, positive constants $c,C$, a nonprincipal Dirichlet character $\chi$ of modulus $q_\chi$, and a finite set $E$ of primes. Suppose that, for each $x$, $h_x$ is a 1-bounded multiplicative function satisfying $$h_x(p)=\chi(p)\qquad(p\leq x^{1/J},\ p\notin E).$$ With $X=cx$, one has $$\label{eq:labels-character-distance}
 \inf_{|t|\leq CX}\mathbb D(h_x,n^{it};X)^2\longrightarrow\infty
 \qquad(x\to\infty).$$ The assertion is uniform over all the functions $h_x$ with this prime agreement. The threshold for $x$ may depend on $J,c,C,\chi,E$.

For each fixed nonnegative integer $H$ and either of the above values of $z$, there are real constants $c_j(z)$ such that, for $Y\ge3$, $$\sum_{n\le Y}g_z(n)
 =Y\sum_{j=0}^{H}c_j(z)(\log Y)^{z-1-j}
       +O_H\bigl(Y(\log Y)^{z-2-H}\bigr),$$ where $$c_0(z)=\frac1{\Gamma(z)}
          \prod_p(1+z/p)(1-1/p)^z>0.$$ For each fixed $C,D>0$, uniformly over nonprincipal Dirichlet characters $\chi$ of modulus $d\le(\log Y)^C$, one has $$\sum_{n\le Y}g_z(n)\chi(n)\ll_{C,D}Y(\log Y)^{-D}.$$ The constants in the second estimate, and the threshold after which it holds, need not be effective.

Fix $D>0$. There is a fixed integer $H=H(D)$ and real coefficients $C_{j,z}(P_0)$, $0\le j\le H$, such that uniformly for $B^{0.89}\le\log Y\le4B$, $$\sum_{n\le Y}g_{z,P_0}(n)
 =Y\sum_{j=0}^{H}C_{j,z}(P_0)(\log Y)^{z-1-j}
       +O_D(YB^{-D}).$$ There is an absolute constant $C_0$ such that for every fixed $j$, $$|C_{j,z}(P_0)|\ll_j(\log P_0)^{C_0+j},\qquad
 C_{0,z}(P_0)=c_0(z)\prod_{p\le P_0}(1+z/p)^{-1}
       \sim\frac{e^{-\gamma_{\rm E}z}}{\Gamma(z)}(\log P_0)^{-z},$$ where $\gamma_{\rm E}$ is Euler’s constant. Uniformly for nonprincipal $\chi$ of modulus $d\le B^{15}$ in the same size range, $$\sum_{n\le Y}g_{z,P_0}(n)\chi(n)\ll_D YB^{-D}.$$

Uniformly for $0<|j|\le T$ and squarefree rough $b\asymp TX$, $$\sum_{\substack{c\asymp X\\b+jc\asymp TX,\ b+jc>0}}
               A_0(c)A_0(b+jc)\ll X\Sigma(j).
 \label{eq:6}$$ On the same fixed log-size ranges, $$\sum_{\substack{b\asymp TX,\ c\asymp X\\a=b+jc\asymp TX,\ a>0}}
               A_0(b)A_0(a)A_0(c)\ll TX^2\Sigma(j).
 \label{eq:7}$$ The implied constants do not depend on $b,j,X$, or $B$.

There is $c_5>0$, independent of all sufficiently large fixed $C_*$, for which the nonnegative energy below satisfies the displayed lower bound: $$\label{eq:15}
\begin{gathered}
 I_{2,x}
 = \frac1x\sum_{c\ge1}
   \rho_0(\log c/B)A(c)
   \sum_{m\ge1}K(m)
   \left|
     \sum_{\substack{a\ge1\\c\mid am+1}}
     A(a)K(l_a)\psi(a/(Tc))
     \phi(am/x)F_x(am+1)
   \right|^2,
 \\
 \liminf_{B\to\infty}\ \liminf_{x\to\infty}
       \frac{I_{2,x}}{BT}\ge c_5
\end{gathered}$$ Its diagonal contribution is negligible: $$\lim_{B\to\infty}\ \limsup_{x\to\infty}
       \frac{I_{2,x}^{\mathrm{diag}}}{BT}=0.$$ Here $I_{2,x}^{\mathrm{diag}}$ denotes the terms with equal coefficient indices on expanding the square, and $l_a=(am+1)/c$ as in <a href="#eq:10" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:10">[eq:10]</a>.

For every possible endpoint type $S_i$, not merely almost every typical type, and every allowed lag $j=k-i$, $$\label{eq:23}
 \mathbb E_{S_k}\mathcal L_{ik}(S_i,S_k;s)
 \ll\frac{\Sigma(j)}T.$$ In particular, every conditional expected degree is $O(1)$, and $\mathbb E\sum_{i,k}\mathcal L_{ik}=O(M)$.

Let $\mathcal M_{ik}(S_i,S_k;s)$ be a symmetric real kernel, zero on nonpairs, with total absolute mass $O(B^{10})$ uniformly in its arguments. Then $$\label{eq:21}
\begin{split}
 &\mathbb E_u\left\|
  \bigl(\mathcal K_{k-i}(u+i,s)
  -\mathcal M_{ik}(S_i(u),S_k(u);s)\bigr)_{i,k}
  \right\|_\square\\
 &\hspace{15pt}\le o(1)+
 \mathbb E_{(S_i)\,\mathrm{indep}}
 \left\|\bigl(\mathcal L_{ik}(S_i,S_k;s)
                   -\mathcal M_{ik}(S_i,S_k;s)\bigr)_{i,k}\right\|_\square.
\end{split}$$ The error is uniform for $s\in\mathcal S$.

For a sufficiently fine fixed regularity grid and then a sufficiently small fixed tolerance, the independent-site latent kernel satisfies $$\sum_{k\ne i}\mathbb E\,\mathcal L_{ik}^{2}\ll B^{-.21}
 \qquad (1\le i\le M).
 \label{eq:24}$$ Moreover, its normalized total mass has bounded second moment: $$\mathbb E\left(\frac1M\sum_{i,k}\mathcal L_{ik}\right)^2\ll1.$$ For every set $I\subseteq\{1,\ldots,M\}$, the same estimates hold after replacing $\mathcal L_{ik}$ by $\mathbf 1_{\{i,k\in I\}}\mathcal L_{ik}$; the normalization remains $M$.

The grid and tolerance can be chosen so that, uniformly for $(a,b,c)\in\mathcal C_j$ and $\eta T\le |j|\le T$, $$\mathbb E^{\mathrm{tilt}}_{a,b}
   \big[
    \mathbf 1_{\mathcal T_b\ \mathrm{regular}}
    \mathbf 1_{\mathcal T_a\ \mathrm{regular}}
    N_{a,b,c}(\mathcal T_b,\mathcal T_a)
   \big]
 \ll B^{.009}.$$

For every fixed coarse partition, uniformly for $1\le d\le B$, $$\label{eq:31}
 \|U_d\|\ll 1,\qquad
 \|(1-P_{\rm av})U_d\|\ll B^{-c_7},
 \qquad c_7=\frac1{200}.$$ Moreover, for every $\epsilon_1>0$, one can choose $m_1$ such that $$\label{eq:32}
 \limsup_{B\to\infty}\ 
 \sup_{\|g\|_2\le1}\|u_g-P_{m_1}u_g\|_2\le\epsilon_1 .$$ All constants are independent of the input $g$. The sufficiently large threshold for $B$ may depend on the chosen fixed partition.

Let $k=i+j$ be an allowed pair with $j>0$, and let $\lvert g\rvert,\lvert h\rvert\le1$. Replacing the coefficient and remaining-site regularity restrictions in $\mathbb E[\mathcal L_{ik}g(S_i)h(S_k)]$ by no restrictions, while keeping the scalar $k_B$, incurs an absolute error at most $$\label{eq:33}
 C\frac{\Sigma(j)}{T}
       \big(r_B+e^{-c_3C_*}\big),\qquad r_B\longrightarrow0.$$ Here $r_B$ may depend on the fixed regularity parameters and on $C_*$, but the constant $C$ is independent of $C_*$, $\eta$, and $C_6$. The pairwise gcd restrictions may then be removed at an additional error $O(B^{-998})$. The resulting integral is $$\label{eq:34}
 k_B B\sum_{a-b=jc}\gamma_g(b)\gamma_h(a)A_0(c)
       \rho_0(\log c/B)
       \Psi_s\big(a/(Tc),b/(Tc)\big).$$ The sum is over positive integers $a,b,c$.

For the matrix $\mathcal M$ in <a href="#eq:43" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:43">[eq:43]</a>, put $\mathcal E_{ik}=\mathcal L_{ik}-\mathcal M_{ik}$, with all three matrices zero on nonpairs. Given $\epsilon_1>0$, choose the coarse partition as in <a href="#eq:32" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:32">[eq:32]</a>. Then $$\label{eq:44}
 \sup_{\substack{g_i,h_k:\Omega_B\to[-1,1]\\
                  g_i,h_k\ \text{real measurable}}}
 \frac1M\left|
  \sum_{i,k}\mathbb E_{S_i,S_k}
      [\mathcal E_{ik}(S_i,S_k;s)g_i(S_i)h_k(S_k)]\right|
 \le C e^{-c_3C_*}+C_\eta\epsilon_1+o(1).$$ The $o(1)$ is uniform in $s$ and in all the indicated tests. The coefficient of $e^{-c_3C_*}$ is independent of $\eta$, $C_6$, and $C_*$. In addition, for every realization of the site types, $$\label{eq:kernel-main-row-bounds}
 \sup_i\sum_k|\mathcal M_{ik}|\ll_{\eta,m_1}1,
 \qquad
 \sup_i\sum_k|\mathcal M_{ik}|^2\ll_{\eta,m_1}T^{-1}.$$

Fix a deterministic subset $J'$ of size $m_2$ and a deterministic assignment $\sigma\in\{-1,1\}^{J'}$. Condition on its site types $\mathbf S_{J'}=\mathbf t$. For $i\in R'$, define the fixed single-site function $$g_i(u)=\mathop{\mathrm{sgn}}\left(\sum_{j\in J'}
                 \mathcal E_{ij}(u,t_j;s)\sigma_j\right).$$ Then, writing $$Z_{J',\sigma,\mathbf t}
   =\frac1M\sum_{k\in R'}
       \left|\sum_{i\in R'}\mathcal E_{ik}(S_i,S_k;s)g_i(S_i)\right|,$$ we have $\mathbb E[Z_{J',\sigma,\mathbf t}\mid\mathbf S_{J'}=\mathbf t]
\le E_0+o(1)$, uniformly in $J'$, $\sigma$, $\mathbf t$, and $s$.

For a fixed prime cutoff $P_1\ge2$, define on all integers $j$ $$\mathfrak S_{P_1}(j)
 =\prod_{\substack{p\le P_1\\p\mid j}}(1-1/p)^{-1}
   \prod_{\substack{p\le P_1\\p\nmid j}}
       \left(1-\frac1{(p-1)^2}\right).$$ This is a bounded nonnegative function of period $D=\prod_{p\le P_1}p$. Moreover, $$\lim_{P_1\to\infty}\ 
   \sup_{\substack{T\in\mathbb N\\T\ge1}}\frac1T
       \sum_{1\le j\le T}
         |\mathfrak S(j)-\mathfrak S_{P_1}(j)|=0,$$ with the supremum taken over positive integers $T$.
